.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (no arguments) | . | <directory>
Launch a login shell ($SHELL -l) in a screen session. If a directory is given,
the shell starts in that directory. If a shell session already exists, reattaches to it.
.TP
.B <cmd> [args]
Shorthand: if the first argument is not a known subcommand or directory, it is treated as a
command to launch or attach to. Equivalent to using
.B a <cmd> [args].
.TP
.B a|attach [cmd] [args]
Create a new screen session running <cmd>, or attach to an existing one.
If no command is given, uses the current directory basename as session name
(same as running screen-new with no arguments).
All additional arguments are passed to the command.
.TP
.B ls|list
List all active screen-new sessions.
.TP
.B kill [name|all]
Kill a specific session by command name, kill all sessions, or list sessions if no argument given.
.TP
.B d|detach [name]
Detach a specific session by command name, or list sessions if no argument given.
.TP
.B edit [name]
Open the session config for <name> in $EDITOR. If no name given, open the main settings.conf.
Creates the session config if it does not yet exist.
.TP
.B switch|sw <name>
Detach the current session (if attached) and attach to the target session.
.TP
.B rename|mv <old> <new>
Rename a live session using
.B screen -X sessionname.
Direct port of tmux-new's session rename.
.TP
.B clone|copy <source> <target>
Clone a session's configuration files to a new session name.
.B This copies configuration only \(em it does NOT clone live window/pane state.
The source session's config and windows files are duplicated and path placeholders
rewritten for the target name, then the target session is launched.
.TP
.B status|info
List every active screen-new session by parsing
.B screen -ls
output. Unlike tmux-new, there is no multi-socket scoping.
.TP
.B show
Show information about the session the current shell is attached to (reads
.B $STY ).
.TP
.B create <name> [window:cmd] ...
Create a custom named session. Each additional argument is a window spec in the form
.I name:command
(or just
.I name
for a login shell). Direct port of tmux-new's create subcommand, using
.B screen -t <name> <index> <cmd>
lines written into a generated per-session windows file.
.TP
.B update template|sessions|all
Refresh the master screen template from source
.RI ( template ),
regenerate every existing session's main config file from the current template
.RI ( sessions ),
or do both
.RI ( all ,
default).
.TP
.B ai|single|shell|dev|go|rust|python|devops|monitoring|database|docker|rpm|node|bun|deno|build|ssh|productivity|test|default
Launch one of the 19 named session presets, direct ports of tmux-new's per-preset
window/command tables written as
.B screen -t <name> <idx> <cmd>
lines in a generated windows file. Additional positional arguments override the
preset's default window specs in order.

.SH KEY BINDINGS
Keybindings are tmux-compatible. PREFIX defaults to Ctrl+n.
.SS Window Management
.TP
.B PREFIX t
Create a new window (login shell).
.TP
.B PREFIX c
Create a new window (login shell, classic binding).
.TP
.B PREFIX n
Next window.
.TP
.B PREFIX p
Previous window.
.TP
.B PREFIX ,
Rename current window.
.TP
.B PREFIX w
Interactive window list.
.TP
.B PREFIX @
Kill current window.
.TP
.B Shift+Left / Shift+Right
Previous / next window (no prefix needed).
.SS Split / Layout
.TP
.B PREFIX \e
Split vertically (side by side).
.TP
.B PREFIX /
Split horizontally (top/bottom).
.TP
.B PREFIX Tab
Interactive window list (choose window).
.TP
.B PREFIX x
Remove current region.
.TP
.B PREFIX Q
Remove all regions except current.
.TP
.B PREFIX h/j/k/l
Resize current region.
.TP
.B PREFIX Left/Right/Up/Down
Focus adjacent region (does not conflict with h/j/k/l resize).
.TP
.B PREFIX +
Zoom current region to full size. Degraded vs tmux: one-way only, use
.B PREFIX Q
to restore the layout.
.TP
.B PREFIX \`
Send a literal backtick, or switch to the other region.
.SS Clipboard
.TP
.B PREFIX [
Enter copy/scrollback mode.
.TP
.B PREFIX ]
Paste from the copy buffer.
.TP
.B PREFIX Y
Copy the screen copy-buffer out to the system clipboard (wl-copy/xclip/pbcopy).
.TP
.B PREFIX Ctrl+v
Paste from the system clipboard into the current window (wl-paste/xclip/pbpaste).
.SS Mouse
.TP
.B PREFIX m
Enable mouse tracking (scroll only).
.TP
.B PREFIX M
Disable mouse tracking.
.SS Session / Misc
.TP
.B PREFIX d
Detach from the session.
.TP
.B PREFIX PREFIX
Send a literal prefix key to the application.
.TP
.B PREFIX R
Reload this session's config live.
.TP
.B PREFIX ?
Show full keybinding reference.

.SH ZOMBIE MODE
When a process inside a screen window exits, the window stays open.
Press
.B k
to kill the window or
.B r
to restart the process.
This allows you to see output or errors before the window disappears.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display the full help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display current version.
.TP
.B \-\-config
Create user settings file.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Reset config file to defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-options
Show all available options.
.TP
.B \-\-dir <path>
Set the working directory.
.TP
.B \-\-name <name>
Set a custom session name instead of using the command basename.
Tab completion suggests existing session names.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Output debugging information.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Removes all formatting.
.TP
.B \-\-silent
Suppress informational output.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Force the operation.
.TP
.B \-\-exec <git|weather|uptime|keybindings>
Run an exec command (git status, weather, uptime, or show keybindings).

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B screen-new
Launch a login shell in a screen session, or attach if already running.
.TP
.B screen-new .
Launch a login shell in the current directory.
.TP
.B screen-new /tmp
Launch a login shell in /tmp.
.TP
.B screen-new htop
Launch htop in a screen session, or attach if already running.
.TP
.B screen-new htop \-d 5
Launch htop with a 5-second delay argument.
.TP
.B screen-new ls
List all active screen-new sessions.
.TP
.B screen-new kill htop
Kill the htop session.
.TP
.B screen-new kill all
Kill all screen-new sessions.
.TP
.B screen-new d htop
Detach the htop session remotely.
.TP
.B screen-new edit htop
Edit the htop session's screen configuration.
.TP
.B screen-new edit
Edit the main settings.conf.
.TP
.B screen-new claude --name ai-scripts --dir /projects
Run claude with custom session name "ai-scripts" in /projects directory.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/myscripts/screen-new/settings.conf
User configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/myscripts/screen-new/sessions/sn-<name>.conf
Per-session screen configuration files.
.TP
.I ~/.config/myscripts/screen-new/keybindings.txt
Key bindings reference file (shown with PREFIX ? inside a session).

